KNOWNBUG: std::erase_if over a populated std::vector still leaves the size
assertion failing (the vector keeps all three elements), but for a *different*
reason than before -- the earlier overload-resolution gap is now fixed.

Progress so far:
  * The convert_function return-type exception-safety crash is fixed and
    regression-tested by cpp20_erase_if_return_type.
  * The iterator -> const_iterator user-defined conversion (via
    __normal_iterator's converting constructor template, whose parameter is the
    class-template-id __normal_iterator<_Iter, _Container>) is now applied
    during member-call overload resolution, so std::erase_if's body
    `__cont.erase(__niter_wrap(...), __cont.end())` converts and runs -- there
    is no longer a "found no match for symbol 'erase'" error, and the
    header-free analogue cpp20_member_overload_converting_ctor is now CORE.
  * Single-element vector::erase(const_iterator) correctly shrinks a populated
    vector (verified separately).

Remaining functional gap (newly exposed now that erase_if compiles and runs):
the *two-iterator range* erase, vector::erase(const_iterator first,
const_iterator last) -- which erase_if uses -- is a no-op.  A direct
`v.erase(v.begin(), v.begin() + 1)` on {1,2,3} likewise leaves size() == 3,
whereas single-element erase and iterator arithmetic (e.g.
`(v.begin()+1) - v.begin() == 1`, `v.end() - v.begin() == 3`) are correct.  The
gap is in the range _M_erase element-shift / _M_finish update (the std::move of
[last, end) into first and the trailing size adjustment), independent of
overload resolution.

Bounded with --unwind to keep this KNOWNBUG fast (the genuine erase_if is now
heavy for BMC).  Reclassify CORE once the range vector::erase(first, last)
shifts the tail and shrinks _M_finish so the populated vector shrinks and
size() == 2 holds.
